Major CommentsWhile the analytical framework is elegant, several foundational claims require rigorous thermodynamic and mathematical clarification before meeting the highly stringent criteria of PRL:Thermodynamic Justification of the Lagrangian: The transition from local incommensurability to a network-level dimensionless fractional excess relies heavily on "Axiom 3: Thermodynamic Linearity". While the author argues that the stoichiometric linearity of oxidative phosphorylation necessitates this, equating the evolutionary penalty of discrete ATP consumption directly with the fractional network cost assumes that biological selection pressure operates uniformly on normalized global dissipation. A formal derivation bridging non-equilibrium thermodynamics (e.g., minimum entropy production) to this specific evolutionary selection rule is needed.  Womersley Transition Threshold: The derivation of the critical Womersley number $Wo_c \approx 3.00$ rests on setting the real part of the Womersley velocity profile correction to $C(Wo_c) = 1/2$. The assertion that this half-value condition represents a state of "maximum regime uncertainty" or maximum entropy is physically intuitive but phenomenological. The transition from parabolic to plug flow lacks a true singularity; treating it analogous to a phase transition requires a more rigorous statistical mechanics justification, especially since the critical body mass prediction $M^*$ scales with the fourth power of this exact threshold.  Assumption of Volumetric Isometry: The generalization of metabolic scaling adopts global volumetric isometry ($V \propto M^1$) as an independent empirical postulate rather than a derived mathematical consequence. Given that the original WBE model sought to derive this scaling purely from fractal geometry, elevating it to an empirical boundary condition weakens the purely "first-principles" claim of the generalized metabolic scaling theorem. The author must explicitly address this epistemological shift.  Coherence vs. Incoherence in Wave Reflections: The multiplicative, incoherent model for wave cost accumulation $(1 - |\Gamma|^2)^G$ isolates the topological branching penalty. Although the transfer-matrix supplemental analysis confirms proportionality and a negligible minimax shift ($|\Delta\alpha^*| < 0.01$), the reliance on a purely uncoupled reflection model in a system where $kL \ll 1$ (high phase coherence) remains conceptually risky. The main text should integrate the transfer-matrix findings more robustly to preclude immediate objections from fluid dynamicists.  Minor CommentsAsymmetry: Real arterial networks are highly asymmetric. While Corollary 5 addresses flow asymmetry for single junctions, the network Lagrangian assumes a uniform, symmetric branching depth $G$. A brief discussion on how a highly "chirped" or asymmetric lattice modifies the emergent stiffness ratio $\kappa_{eff}$ is warranted.  Nomenclature and Modularity: The submissions are split across several interconnected papers (Paper I, II, III, etc.). For PRL, the core minimax theorem, the wall-scaling symmetry breaking, and the Kleiber derivation must be condensed into a single 3,000-word letter, with the exhaustive functional proofs relegated to Supplemental Material or a simultaneous Physical Review E submission.  Approximations: The thin-wall approximation ($h \ll r$) is explicitly noted to lose accuracy at arteriolar scales, reaching $h/r \approx 0.42$. The bounds of the integration and their perturbative limits in this terminal regime should be tightened. 